  "raw": " Tito became an outspoken, public advocate for reforms of the communist system.\n\nAhead of the uprising in the Soviet Union and Poland, he helped to form the Communist Party of Yugoslavia. In 1922, after the death of the communist leader, Vladimir Lenin, Tito became the leader of this new Communist Party.\n\nIn May, 1923, Tito was elected chairman of the Communist Party (Zapadnosti) and became the Prime Minister of Yugoslavia. In 1928 he was renamed ‘President’ of the republic. In the Soviet Union he rose through the chain to become the General Secretary of the Communist Party .\n\nTito and Vladimir Lenin\n\nTito had previously worked in Germany, Sweden and Switzerland. At the time, he was also employed by the USSR’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs.\n\nIn 1924, a Bolshevik coup took place in Russia with the Soviet Union taking possession of the government.\n\nOn his return to Yugoslavia, Tit",
  "serialized": "Tito became an outspoken, public advocate for reforms of the communist system. Ahead of the uprising in the Soviet Union and Poland, he helped to form the Communist Party of Yugoslavia. In 1922, after the death of the communist leader, Vladimir Lenin, Tito became the leader of this new Communist Party. In May, 1923, Tito was elected chairman of the Communist Party (Zapadnosti) and became the Prime Minister of Yugoslavia. In 1928 he was renamed ‘President’ of the republic. In the Soviet Union he rose through the chain to become the General Secretary of the Communist Party . Tito and Vladimir Lenin Tito had previously worked in Germany, Sweden and Switzerland. At the time, he was also employed by the USSR’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs. In 1924, a Bolshevik coup took place in Russia with the Soviet Union taking possession of the government. On his return to Yugoslavia, Tit",
